Z||000000||grinthock||04-21-2001||07:44 AM||||Anyone have any ideas?  MY speedo is out.  But it's not out the sme all the time, it slowly get's WORSE (it's over stating)  here is a example<P>at 40 it shows 40<BR>at 50 it shows 50<BR>at 60 it shows 60<BR>at 78 it shows 80<BR>at 120 it shows 111<BR>at 130 it shows 121<P>I confirmed all my speeds via GPS Satelite Tracking.<P>any ideas?||||reg||4
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||04-21-2001||07:54 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by grinthock:<BR><B>Anyone have any ideas?  MY speedo is out.  But it's not out the sme all the time, it slowly get's WORSE (it's over stating)  here is a example<P>at 40 it shows 40<BR>at 50 it shows 50<BR>at 60 it shows 60<BR>at 78 it shows 80<BR>at 120 it shows 111<BR>at 130 it shows 121<P>I confirmed all my speeds via GPS Satelite Tracking.<P>any ideas?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Be sure your GPS is designed to go that fast...<BR>If it is not certified aircraft GPS, it could be off at high speeds...<BR>If the GPS isn't suspect, go to a speedo shop and get your speedo calibrated...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000002||grinthock||04-21-2001||09:00 AM||||My GPS is certified for that speed (upto 500km/hr) it's a Garmin StreetPilot and I know it's always BANG ON accurate because I drive 2 other vehicles (plus 1 other fiero) and it's always bang on.   The only thing I havn't tried yet is connecting my laptop to the Diag Connector and seeing if the ECM is seeing the right speed (in otherwords is it the gauge or the VSS)||||reg||4
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||04-21-2001||09:02 AM||||GPS shows only STRAIGHT LINE speeds. If at any time you were going up or down hill and changing elevation, this WILL affect your straight line speed. Take a look at where you're running, even the slightest difference will effect your readings.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Jay||04-21-2001||09:21 AM||||If your other cars are reading okay, why not drive one alongside the fiero?  I know it's not as accurate as a GPS but it will tell you if you are close enough for it to matter.  Sometimes we get all caught up in technology and forget that in many cases the simple way may be the best way.  Just a thought.<P>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT <A HREF="http://www.members.home.net/jaygrande" TARGET=_blank>www.members.home.net/jaygrande</A> <BR>||||reg||7
Z||000005||Bob Englert||04-21-2001||10:43 AM||||IT is very common for speedometers to be off a little.  I would be very happy if my speedometer tracked that well.  Small changes such as brand of replacement tires can affect the calibration.<P>A speedometer shop can check it on a dynomometer and give you a printout to see how far off it is.  I doubt there is anything you can do to improve it.<BR> <P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
